---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayCategoryHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'Rural EB-5 | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'Rural EB-5 covers reserved-category visa set-asides, how rural projects differ from urban TEA projects in practice, common project types, and what to evaluate before committing capital.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/rural-eb5`;
const h1 = 'Rural EB-5';
const subhead =
  'What reserved-category rural EB-5 means, how investors compare it with urban projects, and what to evaluate before selecting a project.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'Choosing a Rural EB-5 Regional Center',
    url: '/research/choosing-rural-eb5-regional-center-guide',
    description: 'How to evaluate rural regional centers and project sponsors.',
  },
  {
    title: '19 Questions for Rural EB-5 Project Due Diligence',
    url: '/research/19-questions-rural-eb5-project-due-diligence',
    description: 'A structured question list for rural project diligence.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayCategoryHero('project-due-diligence');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for rural investment"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About Rural EB-5"
  breadcrumbTitle="Rural EB-5"
  keywords={[
    'Rural EB-5',
    'EB-5 reserved category',
    'rural EB-5 vs urban',
    'EB-5 visa set-aside',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      EB-5 investment involves financial risk, including potential loss of principal, and does not guarantee a green
      card, visa availability, or any specific processing time. Reserved-category designation is one factor among many
      and does not assure immigration or investment outcomes. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a
      FINRA-registered financial adviser for guidance specific to your situation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2>What rural / reserved-category EB-5 means</h2>
  <p>
    Under the EB-5 Reform and Integrity Act framework, a portion of EB-5 immigrant visas is set aside in reserved
    categories, including projects in rural areas and certain other designated types such as infrastructure. Rural
    reserved-category projects must meet statutory geographic definitions (generally areas outside metropolitan
    statistical areas, or within certain population and distance criteria defined in the program rules). The set-aside
    structure is intended to allocate a share of visa numbers to those categories separately from the unreserved pool.
    Whether that allocation may offer different visa timing for a given country of birth depends on demand, chargeability,
    and current Visa Bulletin movement (not on marketing claims). Confirm current category availability with an
    immigration attorney and the
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Rural EB-5 vs. Urban EB-5</h2>
  <p>
    Investors often compare rural reserved-category projects with urban Targeted Employment Area (TEA) projects that
    use the same reduced investment threshold but draw from different visa-allocation pools. Practical differences
    people weigh include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Visa allocation.</strong> Rural reserved visas are drawn from the reserved set-aside; many urban TEA
      projects use the unreserved (standard) allocation. Relative wait times can differ by country of birth and change
      over time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Typical project types.</strong> Rural pipelines often include hospitality, infrastructure-adjacent, and
      real estate developments tied to non-metro locations; urban TEA projects more often sit inside designated
      high-unemployment metro areas.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Geographic requirements.</strong> Rural eligibility turns on statutory rural definitions; urban TEA
      eligibility turns on TEA designation rules. Both still require lawful source of funds, job creation, and full
      petition documentation.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Neither structure is automatically preferable. The right choice depends on the investor's chargeability, risk
    tolerance, project diligence findings, and immigration goals. Discuss tradeoffs with counsel before committing
    capital.
  </p>

  <h2>Rural EB-5 for specific situations</h2>
  <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
    <a
      href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-investors"
      class="block rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-150"
    >
      <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Indian investors</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0 text-sm" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        India backlog context is why many Indian nationals weigh reserved-category timing carefully.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a
      href="/pathways/eb5-concurrent-filing"
      class="block rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-150"
    >
      <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Concurrent filing</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0 text-sm" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        Concurrent filing applies to rural EB-5 the same way it does for other qualifying EB-5 projects.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a
      href="/pathways/eb5-source-of-funds"
      class="block rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-150"
    >
      <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Source of funds</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0 text-sm" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        Lawful source and path-of-funds standards apply regardless of rural or urban project choice.
      </p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>Common rural project types</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Hotel / hospitality.</strong> Lodging and related hospitality developments appear frequently among rural
      EB-5 offerings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Infrastructure.</strong> Some projects are structured around infrastructure-designated activity under
      program rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real estate.</strong> Residential, mixed-use, or commercial real estate in rural-qualifying locations is
      another common category.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Naming these categories describes what investors often see in the market; it is not an endorsement of any project
    type, sponsor, or offering.
  </p>

  <h2>What to evaluate</h2>
  <p>
    Before committing capital to a rural project, investors typically examine sponsor track record, I-956F status,
    capital-stack position, job-creation methodology, business-plan assumptions, and how the rural geographic
    qualification is documented. For a full question list rather than a shortened summary here, see
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-project-due-diligence">EB-5 project due diligence</a> and the research guides
    <a href="/research/19-questions-rural-eb5-project-due-diligence">19 questions for rural EB-5 project due diligence</a>
    and
    <a href="/research/choosing-rural-eb5-regional-center-guide">choosing a rural EB-5 regional center</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
